<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcus Furius Camillus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 365 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marcus Furius Camillus was a Roman statesman and general of the early fourth century BCE. He captured the Etruscan city of Veii in 396 BCE after a long siege, and Roman tradition credited him with saving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after the Gallic sack of about 390 BCE. He was celebrated as a second founder of Rome, though much of his story is legendary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-furius-camillus.jpg" alt="Marcus Furius Camillus" width="304" height="609" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: see filename or category · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domenico_ghirlandaio,_affreschi_della_sala_dei_gigli_02_furio_camillo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wars with the Aequi, Volsci and the Etruscan city of Veii filled the fifth century, and Veii fell to <b>Marcus Furius Camillus</b> in 396 BCE, doubling Roman territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Veii fell in 396 BCE after a long siege led by <b>Marcus Furius Camillus</b>; Tarquinia fought Rome in the 350s BCE; Etruscan cities backed the Samnites and Gauls defeated at Sentinum in 295 BCE; and Rome won again at Lake Vadimo in 283 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Marcus Furius Camillus</b> dedicated a temple on the Aventine Hill to &quot;Juno, Queen of Veii&quot;, vowed on his conquest of Veii, and placed in it a statue of the goddess brought from that city.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-1">1 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The city usually allied with Veii against Rome; Roman legend tells how <b>Camillus</b>, besieging it in 394 BCE, sent back a schoolmaster who tried to hand over his pupils, and the Faliscans surrendered in admiration.</blockquote><a href="/state/falerii">Falerii</a></li><li><blockquote>In 396 BCE, after a siege said to have lasted ten years, <b>Camillus</b> took the city, reportedly by tunnelling under its walls.</blockquote><a href="/state/veii">Veii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-375" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marcus Furius Camillus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-375">What was here in 375 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
